                "sessionId": "2026-07-03-inside-the-uc-session",
                "speakerRef": "Amrita",
                "tStart": 262,
                "tEnd": 297,
                "text": "Awesome. So, all of you can pat your back for getting the answers right. Um, and I think what we'll do in the interest of time is skip the next poll, maybe we'll bring it up back later when we're, you know, but I just want to, you know, Aastha, Beauty, and Vaishnavi hand it to you to lead in, you know, to start the session with the overview of the UC system, and then we move into the application, and then particularly the PIQs and, you know, that's the order in which we are going to go over today's session. Thank you."
              },
              "textSha256": "1159fc6a68bf0cf19fb3e5969867f4ad88d3c8d06bd9606f1ee148ec47cd0259"
            }
          ]
        },
        "originalMigration": {
          "available": true,
          "reason": null,
          "artifact": {
            "available": true,
            "path": "data/toc-migrated/2026-07-03-inside-the-uc-session/turns.json",
            "revision": "907c9c9434f474622181bbc179f6ac90d75853b9",
            "sha256": "db2faf243a3402ad77506a455ff8ab21f6bbb32b6b2092bce5f2b16b35d89488",
            "bytes": 76162
          },
          "matches": [
            {
              "record": {
                "_id": "2026-07-03-inside-the-uc-session-t016",
                "tenantId": "toc",
                "sessionId": "2026-07-03-inside-the-uc-session",
                "speakerRef": "unknown",
                "tStart": 353,
                "tEnd": 893,
                "text": "Aastha Chhikara: Perfect. Thank you all so much. So taking us directly into, you know, the part of the presentation that has a lot of learning, you know, involved. So, University of California, like we have, you know, seen in the poll as well, there are 10, you know, campuses. And ideally the University of California, you know, campuses are very beautifully situated across the entire state of California. You can see my mouse moving with the state as well. So there are four UCs in Northern California, there are five UCs in Southern California. Now, the University of California system is a public university system. You have—may have heard of public universities, private universities in the U.S., state universities as well. So the UC system and the focus areas and, you know, what the UCs really stand out for, or why students actually want to come and study at a University of California campus: The gist of that you can see on the right-hand side of your screen. So one of course is academic and research excellence. So when we're talking about even undergraduate education, you have opportunities to do research, you have opportunities to work with faculty, and of course you're essentially studying and learning with faculty that is required to do research while they're teaching students as well. So that's an ongoing process that they're a part of, and you as a student, you know, also have the option, you know, to join them as well. The second thing is our campuses, in terms of the location, they're very nicely spread out, you know, throughout the entire state. While they're spread out, they're also located very close to, you know, hubs that are important for students right from the bachelor's level to start exploring, you know, options in terms of internships and opportunities for themselves as well, right? So the campuses are very diverse, even the programs and the majors that they may study are very, very diverse across the campuses. We have, you know, a link that we'll be sharing, you know, and different links that we'll be sharing with you through the course of this, but at this time please know that you can apply, you know, to so many different majors at any of the campuses all through just this one University of California, you know, application that one student does. So out of these, four UCs you will see up in the north: UC Davis, UC Berkeley, Santa Cruz, and Merced. Now these four UCs are in and around the Silicon Valley area, very ideally situated as well. And then moving further south to Southern California, you have Santa Barbara, you have Los Angeles, Riverside, Irvine, and San Diego. Now these five UCs are actually close to the entertainment industry, which is near Los Angeles; the agricultural industry near Riverside; and also the biotech industry, which is Oceanside area. It's actually in between Irvine, Riverside, and San Diego area. So ideally even in terms of, you know, prospects that students, you know, see for their future as well, there's a lot that you can learn in and around this area. Also, as a University of California student, you get to be part of the fabulous alumni network that you can, you know, use and also, you know, support in the future as well. All of the campuses are, you know, they're not stand-alone buildings; they've been around for over 100 years. Some campuses, you know, even more, and some campuses are, you know, newer campuses as well. But they all offer, you know, very, very vibrant student life. There's a lot of resources in terms of like a shared library that is actually shared between all of the UCs together, even in terms of, you know, extracurriculars or learning opportunities outside of classrooms as well. These, you know, organizations and clubs, you know, that we have to offer on campus, actually are offered at a majority of these campuses. If we feel we don't even find, you know, one organization that I want to be part of, you're very welcome to actually create one with five other students and, you know, an advisor, faculty advisor, you know, as well. All of these campuses are globally recognized. All of the campuses also fall amongst the top 100 institutions in the U.S. according to the U.S. News Report as well. So, yeah, globally recognized institutions, and some of the campuses do also offer scholarships. So this is one big myth that I want to, you know, sort of bust right now and really clarify that the UC system does offer scholarships for international students, out-of-state students as well, and we will talk about that during, you know, our presentation also. So in the interest of time, I want to quickly, you know, now start with institutional introductions, and then we will, you know, take you all through the University of California application. The University of California application, you can see in the center of this slide, these essentially are the only sections on your application. So it is pretty, you know, easy when you look at it like this. When you're filling it out, we recommend that you do it sequentially, just so that you don't miss out on any one of the subsections that does open up in your application. But essentially it's an \"About you\" section in which we learn about you, where you're studying, where the student is from—which city, which country, which place they're from—their family, how many siblings they have, you know, how their parents have been supporting them; different campuses and majors is, you know, what you get to pick and choose—we'll take you through that as well; the academic history; activities and awards; and scholarships—now these are the three and the personal insight, are the three major sections that you will essentially be, you know, focusing on today. So before we start the application, one quick, you know, round of introduction to our campuses. First I'm going to focus on Riverside, that's the institution and the campus that I represent. You can see it in my background as well. So University of California, Riverside is in the Southern California region. We're about an hour east of Los Angeles, about an hour and a half north of Riverside—north of San Diego, in the city called Riverside. Now, one of my favorite parts of having been an international student studying in Southern California is just the access to opportunities where you're studying as an undergrad student. So at Riverside, you actually are in the center where you can essentially reach the beaches, the ski mountains, Disneyland, Palm Springs, Temecula—all of these places within, you know, between 45 minutes to an hour and a half's drive. Even further south down to Mexico in 2 hours. So ideally, in terms of, you know, things that you want to explore with friends, you want to, you know, meet new people... California itself, you know, it's a very, very diverse, you know, state, welcoming students and people from all nationalities. But, yeah, you won't find anything that you would miss essentially in the Southern California region. You'd find a lot of Indian students and Indian families, and, you know, families from around the world, you know, as well. So UC Riverside, I just want to highlight maybe a couple of programs, you know, like our Psychology, our Entomology program, our Physics, Astrophysics concentration area. We are one of the UCs that does offer Business Analytics and Business Administration as well. And even in terms of the support that students do require, so we have a set international, you know, office that will help students, you know, figure out how to navigate coming to a new country. I do want to highlight something: So of course UC campuses do also have Nobel laureates that are teaching students as well. So UC Riverside actually has two Nobel laureates, one in Physics and one in Chemistry, Dr. Barish and Dr. Strock, and they are teaching students biosciences as well. That's our mascot on the very left, Scotty the Bear. And with regards to scholarship, we do cover more than one year's tuition, living expenses, personal expenses with the Academic Merit Scholarship that students can, you know, get as well. So this scholarship is based off of the GPA that students essentially have, and this scholarship goes up to $89,100, and that is spread across your 4 years. So I will now pass on to UC Merced, taking you guys up north."
